{
  "result": "I think a lot about the way empty houses hold temperature. Long after people leave, after the furniture is gone and the curtains are taken down, the rooms somehow remember what it felt like to be warm. You can walk into an abandoned house in August and find one corner that is still cool, as if it's been saving itself. \n\nMy grandmother kept a junk drawer that was not junk at all. It was a museum. Rubber bands from produce, dead batteries, a single earring, three different kinds of tape, a tiny notebook with someone else's grocery list in it that she found at the store and decided to keep. \"Someone took the time to write it,\" she said. \n\nWe are all collecting these drawers, I think. Not just objects, but moments we forgot to throw away. The exact sound the screen door made at your childhood home. The way your best friend in third grade spelled your name wrong every time and you never corrected him. \n\nThere's something quietly defiant about remembering small things when the world wants us to focus on big things. Big headlines, big movements, big futures. But life happens in the margin. In the rubber bands and the cool corners. And if you pay attention there, you're never really bored. You're just surrounded by evidence that you were here, and it mattered, even if only to you. \n\nThat is enough. More than enough, actually, to build a whole life upon if you ask me.",
